    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "393469ad-a6e1-502f-8b31-693cbfb946cc",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186133881/assets/2357fc7c9f3d64b1d917d9cb17fbaf2bc044bf3dc47bfa6b517fb5df8f36794c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $F$ is not irrotational\n\nIV. $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that $F$ represents the velocity field of flowing water. Place a paddlewheel at $P$ with its axis in the $z$-direction. If it rotates, the field has nonzero curl; if it does not, the field has zero curl.\n\nThe arrows produce no local paddlewheel rotation. Thus $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ throughout space. The field is irrotational, and its zero curl points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nWith that in mind, check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n- Statement II is false.\n- Statement III is false.\n- Statement IV is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "085827d3-e716-5915-93d3-7eb10983d98a",
